-- title:
--   Level monotonicity and component-wise compatibility of the specialisation family
-- statement:
--   The setting is the two-chart integral model of $X_1(Mp)$ over a discrete valuation ring above $p$, its special fibre at a place of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and an abstract specialisation family on $J_1(Mp)$.
--
--   Arithmetic data. A valuation subring `Pl` of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; a prime $p$; a natural number $M$ with `hM` : $5 \le M$ and `hpM` : $p \nmid M$; a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, together with $\zeta \in L$ and `hζ` making $\zeta$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity; an intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq L((q))$ with `hK` : $K$ is the field [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the compositum of $L$ with the image of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; a discrete valuation domain $A$ which is an order in $L$ with $L$ as fraction field, with `hAp` : $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and `hζA` : $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$; an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with that on $L$; and $j \in K$ with `hj` : $j$ is the image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), $j$ being nonzero. The residue field $\kappa$ of `Pl` is assumed algebraically closed, of characteristic $p$, and an $A$-algebra. The relevant curve is [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252), the pushout of the two affine charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the integral closures of $A[j]$ and $A[j^{-1}]$ in $K$, taken over $\operatorname{Spec} A$; it is assumed proper.
--
--   Special fibre and its two components. Schemes $C_1, C_2$ with structure morphisms $c_1, c_2$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\kappa$, each proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral; closed immersions $i_1, i_2$ of $C_1, C_2$ over $\kappa$ into the base change of the two-chart model to $\kappa$; `hcover` : every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` : the fibre product of $i_1$ and $i_2$ is reduced; $n$ with `hn` : that fibre product has exactly $n$ points, and `hn0` : $n > 0$. A section $\varepsilon$ of the model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, sections $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ of $c_1, c_2$, and `hε₁` : $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $\kappa$.
--
--   Relative Picard data. A `RelativePic0Designation` $D$ for the model over $A$ (a scheme with structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section), with `hrep` : $D$ represents the subfunctor of rigidified line bundles cut out by the fibrewise algebraic-equivalence-to-zero condition `algEquivZeroCut` at $\varepsilon$, and `hsm`, `hsep` : $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is smooth and separated. Likewise `hreps` for the base change of $D$ to $\kappa$, with `hPk` identifying its Poincaré bundle with the base change of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep`; designations $D_1, D_2$ with `hrep₁`, `hrep₂` for $c_1, c_2$; a morphism $\nu_2$ from the $\kappa$-base change of $D$ to $D_2$ over $\kappa$ with `hν₂` expressing, for every test point, that pulling back the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₂` along $\nu_2$ agrees with restricting to $C_2$ and rigidifying along $\varepsilon_2$; and `hproj` : the two components of `G.proj` are computed by the restriction map attached to $i_1$ (`RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom`) and by $\nu_2$ respectively.
--
--   Geometric fibre and its function field. Algebra and tower hypotheses placing $A$ and $L$ inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hsmL`, `hgiL` : the base change of the model to $L$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral; `hprL`, `hgcL` : the base change of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $L$ is proper and geometrically connected; a `CurveModel` $M_\eta$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) together with an isomorphism $e_\eta$ onto the geometric fibre and `heη` over the base; `Mη_chart_nonempty` and `hMηpin` : the identification of function fields is pinned by $q$-expansions, in that each element of the finite chart algebra has germ corresponding to its Laurent series pushed forward along $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hgal` : for $g \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ fixing $L$ pointwise, the place attached to a point is moved by [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) accordingly.
--
--   Hecke, diamond and Galois structure. `hin` : [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58), and `hcomm` : [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54); a semiring action of $\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ on $A$ with `hΓA` : it is compatible with the action on $L$; a bijection `gpts` from $J_1(Mp) =$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) onto the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D$; endomorphisms $\varphi(t)$ of $D$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ indexed by the Hecke algebra [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16), and semilinear automorphisms $\tau(s)$ for $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$, subject to `hφmul` ($\varphi(t)$ respects the relative group law), `hφpts` ($\varphi$ induces the Hecke action on $J_1(Mp)$ through `gpts`), `hτ1`, `hτmul` ($\tau$ is multiplicative), `hτφ` ($\tau$ and $\varphi$ commute), `hgadd` (`gpts` is additive) and `hτpts` (`gpts` is Galois-equivariant through $\tau$).
--
--   Abel–Jacobi data. `hDL` : representability over $L$; a morphism `ajL` from the $L$-base change of the curve to that of $D$; a comparison morphism `kL` between the geometric and the $L$-fibre, with `hkL₁`, `hkL₂`; a morphism `ajbar` from $M_\eta.C$ to $D.P$ and a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point `εbar` of $M_\eta.C$, with `hPL` (Poincaré comparison over $L$), `hajLε` (`ajL` carries the cusp to the zero section), `hajL` (the defining property: pulling back the Poincaré bundle along a point $x$ composed with `ajL` gives the line bundle of the divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of $\varepsilon$), `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, and `hpts_aj` (for points $x, s$ of $M_\eta.C$ with $s$ the cusp there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $[x]-[s]$ whose class corresponds under `gpts` to $x$ followed by `ajbar`).
--
--   Special-fibre group data. A [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9) datum $G$ (groups `J0s`, `JI`, `JE`, a subgroup `torus`, and a surjection `proj` with kernel `torus`); bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` of `G.J0s`, `G.JI`, `G.JE` with the $\kappa$-points of the $\kappa$-base change of $D$, of $D_1$ and of $D_2$; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` : these bijections turn addition into tensor product of the pulled-back Poincaré bundles; `hPTS` : `pts` is additive for the relative group law of `hreps` and carries $0$ to the unit; and an operator datum $O$ of type [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreOpsV3 G`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePOpsV3.html#L13) with `hO` (four clauses): on $\kappa$-points, `O.hecke ℓ` is induced by $\varphi$ of the Hecke generator at a prime $\ell$, `O.diamondP b` and `O.diamondN d` by $\varphi$ of [`ModularCurve.diamondGen d`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L20) for $d$ coprime to $Mp$ with the prescribed residues modulo $M$ and modulo $p$, and `O.inertia b` by $\tau(s)$ for any $s$ with $s\zeta = \zeta^{b}$.
--
--   Integral points and the Néron model. `hPl` : `Pl` lies over $p$; a ring map $\rho : A \to$ `Pl` with `hρ` lifting $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and `hAlgκ` identifying the $A$-algebra structure on $\kappa$ with reduction of $\rho$. The hypothesis `hF3` (five clauses, summarised here) asserts, for every subgroup $I$ of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ contained in the inertia subgroup of `Pl` over $\mathbb{Q}$, acting trivially on $p$-th roots of unity and of finite index in that inertia subgroup, the existence of a ring map $\rho_I$ from $A$ to $\mathcal{O}_I :=$ `Pl` $\cap \overline{\mathbb{Q}}^{\,I}$ lifting $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, such that, with $\mathrm{dom}$ the set of $x \in J_1(Mp)$ whose point `gpts x` factors through $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}_I$: $I$ fixes $\mathrm{dom}$ pointwise; such factorisations are unique; $\mathrm{dom}$ contains $0$ and is closed under subtraction; for each $n > 0$ prime to $p$, reduction to $\kappa$ is injective on $n$-torsion $\mathcal{O}_I$-points and every $n$-torsion $\kappa$-point lifts to an $n$-torsion $\mathcal{O}_I$-point; and $\mathrm{dom}$ is stable under Frobenius elements at `Pl` normalising $I$. Further, a scheme $\mathcal{A}$ with structure morphism $a$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a morphism $\iota$ over $a$ into $D$, with `h𝒜` (six clauses, summarised here): $\iota$ is a closed immersion; $a$ is proper and smooth with connected geometric fibres; the image of $\mathcal{A}$ is closed under the unit, multiplication and inverse of the relative group law; the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $\mathcal{A}$ are exactly the subgroup [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) of $J_1(Mp)$; and the image is stable under every $\varphi(t)$. Finally `hF10` : for every $\kappa$-algebra $T'$, a $T'$-point of the $\kappa$-base change of $D$ that lifts to $\mathcal{A}$ is annihilated by $\varphi$ of $\sum_{b \in (\mathbb{Z}/p)^{\times}} \langle d_b \rangle$, where $d_b$ is the Chinese-remainder lift of $(b, 1)$ modulo $p$ and $M$.
--
--   Igusa components and Raynaud's description. An integral weight-one form $w$ over $\kappa$ of level $M$ (a modular form of weight $1$ on $\Gamma_1(M)$ with integral $q$-expansion whose reduction is nonzero), defining the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35); curve models $\mathrm{Mdl}_1, \mathrm{Mdl}_2$ over $\kappa$ of that field with isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$, $e_2 : \mathrm{Mdl}_2.C \cong C_2$ over $\kappa$ (`he₁`, `he₂`); `hne₁` and `hgauss₁`, pinning the identification for $C_1$: if $a$ lies in the finite chart algebra and power series $x, y$ over $A$ satisfy $a\,\bar y = \bar x$ in Laurent series over $L$ with $y$ having nonzero reduction, then the germ of $a$ on $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$ has Laurent expansion the quotient of the reductions of $x$ and $y$; `hεC₂` : the reduced cusp section avoids the image of $i_2$; `hεgal` : $\varepsilon$ is equivariant for any automorphism of the two-chart model twisting the base by $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ and acting on the chart algebra by a ring automorphism given on Laurent coefficients by $s$. A finite set `nodesIg` of pairs of places of the Igusa function field, an additive bijection $\Psi$ from `G.J0s` onto [`AlgebraicCurve.GluedPic0 κ`](def/AlgebraicCurve_GluedPic0.html#L201) of the Igusa field along `nodesIg`, additive bijections $\theta_1, \theta_2$ of `G.JI`, `G.JE` with $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the Igusa field, and `hF4c` (seven clauses, summarised here): `nodesIg` is exactly the set of pairs of places cut out by the $\kappa$-points of the intersection of $i_1$ and $i_2$; its cardinality is $n$; both projections are injective on it; [`AlgebraicCurve.GluedPic0.toPic0Pair`](def/AlgebraicCurve_GluedPic0.html#L229) composed with $\Psi$ equals $(\theta_1, \theta_2)$ composed with `G.proj`; the image of `G.torus` under $\Psi$ is the range of [`AlgebraicCurve.GluedPic0.nodeUnit`](def/AlgebraicCurve_GluedPic0.html#L247); and $\theta_1, \theta_2$ are computed by the Abel–Jacobi divisors $[x] - [\varepsilon_1]$, $[x] - [\varepsilon_2]$. A semilinear automorphism `frobT` of the Igusa function field with `hfrobT` : it raises every Laurent coefficient to the $p$-th power.
--
--   The specialisation family. For each admissible quadruple $(I, hI, hI\mu, hIf)$ as above, a subgroup `dom𝒟 I …` of $J_1(Mp)$ and an additive map `sp𝒟 I …` from it to the glued $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ along `nodesIg`, subject to `hIFm` (two clauses): $y$ belongs to `dom𝒟 I …` if and only if `gpts y` factors through $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}_I$ via the map chosen in `hF3`; and `sp𝒟 I … ⟨y, hy⟩` equals $\Psi$ applied to `pts.symm u` whenever $u$ is a $\kappa$-point of the base change of $D$ whose first projection is the reduction of such a factorisation. Finally `hlev` (two clauses): for admissible $I$ and $J$ with $J \le I$, any $\mathcal{O}_I$-factorisation of `gpts y` pushes out to an $\mathcal{O}_J$-factorisation obtained by the inclusion $\mathcal{O}_I \subseteq \mathcal{O}_J$, giving again a factorisation of `gpts y` and the same reduction to $\kappa$; and for $J \le I$ and $y \in$ `dom𝒟 I …` one has $y \in$ `dom𝒟 J …` with `sp𝒟 J … ⟨y, ·⟩ = sp𝒟 I … ⟨y, hy⟩`.
--
--   Conclusion. For all subgroups $I$ and $I'$ of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$, each contained in the inertia subgroup of `Pl` over $\mathbb{Q}$, each acting trivially on all $p$-th roots of unity, and each of finite index in that inertia subgroup, if $I' \le I$, then for every $y$ in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ together with [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) — that is, for every $y \in J_1(Mp)$ — lying in `dom𝒟 I …`, there is a proof that $y$ lies in `dom𝒟 I' …`, and the images under [`AlgebraicCurve.GluedPic0.toPic0Pair`](def/AlgebraicCurve_GluedPic0.html#L229) of `sp𝒟 I' … ⟨y, ·⟩` and of `sp𝒟 I … ⟨y, hy⟩` coincide. Thus the conclusion records monotonicity of the domains in the level together with equality of the specialisations only after projection to the pair of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$-classes on the two Igusa components, not equality in the glued $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ itself.
--
--   This is the monotonicity-and-compatibility clause for the specialisation family attached to the norm-free part of $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$: as the inertia level shrinks, the domain of definition grows and the two component-wise classes of the specialisation are unchanged. It is used in the assembly of the pinned semistable specialisation statement with diamond operators, [`ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div), which feeds the level-lowering step.
